  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nA cold front currently extending from Minnesota to Nebraska will\nsag southeastward this evening, triggering scattered thunderstorms\nacross Wisconsin and Iowa. A few of these cells could potentially\nspill into the N/NW KILX CWA in a weakening phase from late\nevening through the overnight hours. Have included low chance \nPoPs (20-30%) along/north of a Rushville to Bloomington line\naccordingly. The front will continue southeastward on Saturday,\nbut will eventually become parallel with the upper flow and\nstall along the I-64 corridor. A weak bubble of high pressure \nbehind the front will ensure a dry day for most of central \nIllinois, with just a slim chance (20%) for a stray shower/storm\nnear the boundary along/south of I-70 during the afternoon.\n\nAs a nocturnal low-level jet streak strengthens across the\nPlains, a cluster of thunderstorms is expected to form along the\ntrailing end of the boundary Saturday night, then track E/SE \ntoward central Illinois overnight into Sunday morning. There is \nstill wide variation among the 12z models with the FV-3/RRFS \nboth focusing convection further north across Wisconsin...and \nthe HRRR/ECMWF/ICON favoring a more southerly position over \neastern Iowa/northern Missouri by 12z Sun. While the details are\nnot yet fully settled, confidence is growing that a cluster of \nstorms will impact at least parts of central Illinois. Have \ntherefore focused highest PoPs (40-50%) along/north of the I-72 \ncorridor late Saturday night into Sunday morning. Once any \nmorning storms depart, the afternoon will be largely dry and \ndecidedly more humid as dewpoints climb into the middle to upper\n70s. With temperatures well into the 80s to near 90 degrees, \nheat index values will peak in the upper 90s to around 100 \ndegrees by Sunday afternoon.\n\nEven hotter conditions are expected on Monday/Tuesday as upper\nridging shunts the bulk of the convection just north of the KILX\nCWA from northern Iowa across Wisconsin/northern Illinois. Will\nneed to keep a close eye on the situation as mesoscale details \nbecome fine-tuned, but for now it appears both days will be \nmostly dry with highs in the 90s and heat index readings \nflirting with Heat Advisory criteria (105 degrees).\n\nAll model guidance agrees that a stronger short-wave trough will\npush a cold front southward into central Illinois Tuesday night\nthrough perhaps the end of next week. Nocturnal convection will\nlikely blossom along the boundary Tuesday night into Wednesday\nmorning, providing all of central and southeast Illinois with\nhigh PoPs (60-80%).",
